  <main>
    <header class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Six real bugs, one migration, in order</p>
      <h1>New stack migration playbook</h1>
      <p class="dek">Moving an already-live project onto code_my_spec's managed devops stack surfaces the same handful of problems, roughly in the same order, every time. This is what to check first, what each failure actually means, and the exact fix — written from the first migration that hit every one of them.</p>

      <div class="tldr">
        <p class="tldr-label">If you're mid-migration right now</p>
        <p>Work top to bottom. Each phase tends to unblock the next — SSH before networking, networking before registry, registry before the app ever boots against real data. If a deploy is failing and you can't tell why, jump straight to <a href="#logging">Phase 3</a> before guessing.</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section id="preflight" class="phase">
      <div class="phase-head"><span class="phase-tag">Before</span><h2>Pre-flight checklist</h2></div>
      <p class="phase-intro">Fifteen minutes here is what turns the rest of this playbook into a formality instead of an incident.</p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>
          Confirm whether this project already runs somewhere, deployed by hand.
          <span class="why">If yes, assume it has a real, unmanaged database — see <a href="#cause">Phase 4</a> — before the first automated deploy ever runs, not after.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          Confirm the account's current SSH key is actually in <code>authorized_keys</code> on the target box — don't infer it from Hetzner's own key list.
          <span class="why">Hetzner only injects a registered key at server-creation time; rotating the account's key never retroactively updates a box created before the rotation.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          Check every private network already attached to the target server, and which zone each one is actually in.
          <span class="why">A box shared with another project can carry a wrong-zone network from that project's own history — see <a href="#zone">Phase 2</a>.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          List every postgres container already running on the target box, not just the one this pipeline will create.
          <span class="why">A shared box accumulates instances across years of manual deploys — the managed one this pipeline creates is rarely the first.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="hostkey" class="phase">
      <div class="phase-head"><span class="phase-tag">01 · SSH</span><h2>Host-key mismatch</h2></div>
      <div class="problem">
        <p class="problem-title">SSH refuses the box with a host-key error that reads like tampering</p>
        <div class="problem-row symptom">
          <p class="problem-label">Symptom</p>
          <p>Every connection attempt fails with a host-key mismatch, even right after freshly scanning and trusting the host.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-row cause">
          <p class="problem-label">Cause</p>
          <p>SSHKit's own <code>known_hosts</code> reader returns on the <em>first</em> hashed line matching a host, silently discarding every other key type on that line — unlike net-ssh's own equivalent, which collects all of them. Scanning for several key algorithms just gives this bug more to throw away.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-row fix">
          <p class="problem-label">Fix</p>
          <p>Scan and negotiate exactly <strong>one</strong> key family end to end, so there's nothing left for the bug to discard.</p>
          <pre><code>ssh-keyscan -t rsa -T 10 -H &lt;host&gt; &gt;&gt; ~/.ssh/known_hosts</code></pre>
          <p>Pin <code>HostKeyAlgorithms</code> to the same family in <code>~/.ssh/config</code> so negotiation can't drift back to a second type mid-handshake.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="authkeys" class="phase">
      <div class="phase-head"><span class="phase-tag">01 · SSH</span><h2>Password fallback</h2></div>
      <div class="problem">
        <p class="problem-title">Key auth is rejected and the connection falls back to an interactive password prompt</p>
        <div class="problem-row symptom">
          <p class="problem-label">Symptom</p>
          <p>The host-key problem is gone, but auth still fails: server responds <code>allowed methods: publickey,password</code>, then the deploy dies on <code>Errno::ENOTTY</code> trying to prompt for a password that has nowhere to go.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-row cause">
          <p class="problem-label">Cause</p>
          <p>The account's current key was never installed in <code>authorized_keys</code> on this particular box. Hetzner only injects a registered key via cloud-init <em>at server-creation time</em> — rotating the account's stored key never retroactively pushes it onto a server that already existed.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-row fix">
          <p class="problem-label">Fix</p>
          <p>Reach the box with whatever credential already has root — an earlier personal key, a different account secret — verify by fingerprint, then append (never overwrite) the current account key.</p>
          <pre><code>ssh-keygen -lE md5 -f ~/.ssh/candidate_key   # confirm it matches a fingerprint Hetzner shows as already on the box
ssh root@&lt;host&gt; "echo '&lt;current pubkey&gt;' &gt;&gt; ~/.ssh/authorized_keys"</code></pre>
          <div class="note"><strong>This is an account-level fix, not a project-level one.</strong> The key lives in <code>AccountSecrets</code>, one per account — reasoning about "whose key" per project is the wrong frame on a shared box.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="zone" class="phase">
      <div class="phase-head"><span class="phase-tag">02 · Network</span><h2>Wrong-zone network</h2></div>
      <div class="problem">
        <p class="problem-title">Creating a builder or any new resource fails with a zone error</p>
        <div class="problem-row symptom">
          <p class="problem-label">Symptom</p>
          <p>Hetzner refuses to create a new server with <code>networks must be in the same network zone</code>, even though the target server itself is running fine on that exact network today.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-row cause">
          <p class="problem-label">Cause</p>
          <p>A server shared across projects can carry <em>multiple</em> private-network attachments — one from each project's own history. Picking blindly from the first entry gets whichever project happened to attach its network first, not the one matching this server's own location. Hetzner tolerates the mismatch on a server that already holds it, but refuses to create anything new with that same combination.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-row fix">
          <p class="problem-label">Fix</p>
          <p>Match the server's own location against each attached network's actual zone, not position in the list.</p>
          <pre><code>zone = network_zone(server.location)
pick = attached_networks.find { |n| n.zone == zone } || attached_networks.first</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="trust" class="phase">
      <div class="phase-head"><span class="phase-tag">02 · Network</span><h2>Stale registry trust</h2></div>
      <div class="problem">
        <p class="problem-title">The zone fix is deployed, but the deploy still fails on the registry</p>
        <div class="problem-row symptom">
          <p class="problem-label">Symptom</p>
          <p><code>Error response from daemon: ... http: server gave HTTP response to HTTPS client</code> — after the network-zone bug above is already fixed and shipped, and after re-running the step that's supposed to fix exactly this.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-row cause">
          <p class="problem-label">Cause</p>
          <p>A step's "satisfied" verdict can be narrower than everything its run actually does. This platform's <code>verify(:server, …)</code> only checks that the box exists and a database URL is set — it has no way to know the box's docker daemon still trusts the <em>old</em> registry address from before the zone fix. Re-running the step through the ordinary verify-gated path sees both of its own checks still pass, reports satisfied, and never touches the stale daemon config.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-row fix">
          <p class="problem-label">Fix</p>
          <p>Force the step to run unconditionally instead of trusting a satisfied verdict that predates the underlying fix. Every step's run is idempotent, so this costs a few redundant provider calls at worst.</p>
          <pre><code>run_provisioning_step(step: "server", force: true)</code></pre>
          <div class="note">Changes an existing tool's schema — reconnect (<code>/mcp</code>) before the client will offer the new parameter.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="logging" class="phase">
      <div class="phase-head"><span class="phase-tag">03 · Diagnosis</span><h2>Getting a real error out</h2></div>
      <p class="phase-intro">Two separate logging gaps made every failure above harder to diagnose than the fix itself. Both are worth checking for in any deploy pipeline, not just this one.</p>
      <div class="problem">
        <p class="problem-title">A refusal names the phase and exit code, and nothing else</p>
        <div class="problem-row symptom">
          <p class="problem-label">Symptom</p>
          <p><code>Migration failed (exit 1) — the previous version is still serving.</code> No output, no hint what actually failed inside that exit code.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-row cause">
          <p class="problem-label">Cause</p>
          <p>The command's output was captured but never logged — collected, then discarded.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-row fix">
          <p class="problem-label">Fix</p>
          <p>Log the captured output alongside the phase and exit code. Obvious once named; easy to never notice is missing until an incident needs it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="problem">
        <p class="problem-title">The output is logged, but the real error still doesn't show up</p>
        <div class="problem-row symptom">
          <p class="problem-label">Symptom</p>
          <p>The logged <code>output=</code> field is nothing but SSH keepalive and channel-close noise — three refusals in a row, no actual error text anywhere in it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-row cause">
          <p class="problem-label">Cause</p>
          <p>A verbose SSH trace keeps emitting debug lines well after the real failure. A blind tail of the last N bytes captures whatever came <em>last</em>, not whatever mattered — and on a slow trace, that's pure noise.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-row fix">
          <p class="problem-label">Fix</p>
          <p>Anchor on the last known error marker (<code>ERROR (</code>, <code>Error response from daemon</code>) and read forward from there, instead of blindly tailing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="note"><strong>General technique:</strong> server-side diagnostics (network/registry resolution, docker daemon state) run in the platform's own process, not on the remote box — they never flow back through the deploy status a caller sees. When status alone isn't enough, check the platform's own log directly for the same time window.</div>
    </section>

    <section id="symptom" class="phase">
      <div class="phase-head"><span class="phase-tag">04 · Data</span><h2>The symptom</h2></div>
      <p>A project takes its <strong>first successful automated production deploy</strong> through code_my_spec. The app has been live with real users for weeks or months, previously deployed by hand. After the deploy, it looks freshly installed: an empty signup screen, zero rows in every table, no history — even though nothing about the deploy itself failed or logged an error.</p>
      <p>Nothing was destroyed. The app is simply talking to a different database than it was five minutes ago.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cause" class="phase">
      <div class="phase-head"><span class="phase-tag">04 · Data</span><h2>Root cause</h2></div>
      <p>A manually-deployed app frequently already runs against an older, unmanaged postgres instance living on the same box — in our case a docker-compose container named <code>infra-postgres-1</code>, holding a database named plainly <code>broken_oaths_prod</code> under a role <code>broken_oaths_prod_app</code>. No <code>app_</code> prefix, no code_my_spec naming convention — because code_my_spec's automation had no part in creating it.</p>
      <p>code_my_spec's own <code>:server</code> provisioning step has no way to know that instance exists. Its database step (<code>Steps.serve_database/4</code> → <code>Database.serve/3</code>) mints a <strong>new</strong> role and database under the managed convention — <code>app_&lt;slug&gt;_&lt;environment&gt;</code> — on the shared, automation-owned <code>cms-postgres</code> container, then unconditionally overwrites <code>DATABASE_URL</code> to point at it. The first successful deploy is what flips the switch.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="field-table">
          <tr><th>Instance</th><th>Container</th><th>Naming</th><th>Owned by</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Legacy</td><td class="mono">infra-postgres-1</td><td class="mono">broken_oaths_prod</td><td>whoever ran the original manual deploy</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Managed</td><td class="mono">cms-postgres</td><td class="mono">app_broken_oaths_prod</td><td>code_my_spec automation</td></tr>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="detect" class="phase">
      <div class="phase-head"><span class="phase-tag">04 · Data</span><h2>Detect — before assuming data loss</h2></div>
      <p>Confirm the shape of the problem before touching anything. All four checks run on the target box.</p>
      <ol class="steps detect">
        <li>
          <p class="step-title">Look for a second postgres container or volume</p>
          <p>The managed stack only expects <code>cms-postgres</code>. Anything else — ours was <code>infra-postgres-N</code> backed by a volume named <code>infra_pgdata</code> — is a candidate for the real data.</p>
          <pre><code>docker ps -a
docker volume ls</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="step-title">List databases on each candidate</p>
          <p>The real data lives under the <em>old</em> naming convention, never <code>app_&lt;slug&gt;_&lt;env&gt;</code>.</p>
          <pre><code>docker exec &lt;candidate-container&gt; psql -U postgres -c '\l'</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="step-title">Confirm which instance the live container is actually talking to</p>
          <p>Never assume — check the connection's own role name against <code>pg_stat_activity</code> on both instances.</p>
          <pre><code>SELECT usename, datname, client_addr
FROM pg_stat_activity
WHERE usename = '&lt;role-of-interest&gt;';</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="step-title">Compare schema versions</p>
          <p>If the legacy and managed databases report the same migration version, they're the same app codebase's schema and a direct dump/restore will apply cleanly.</p>
          <pre><code>SELECT max(version) FROM schema_migrations;</code></pre>
          <div class="note"><strong>Run this on both.</strong> A mismatch means reconcile the schema before restoring data, not after.</div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="migrate" class="phase">
      <div class="phase-head"><span class="phase-tag">04 · Data</span><h2>Migration procedure</h2></div>
      <p>Move the real data <em>onto</em> the managed instance. Don't repoint <code>DATABASE_URL</code> at the legacy one — see <a href="#principles">the principles</a> below.</p>
      <ol class="steps migration">
        <li>
          <p class="step-title">Back up both databases first — non-negotiable</p>
          <p>Dump the legacy source <em>and</em> the (empty) destination before changing anything.</p>
          <pre><code>pg_dump -U postgres broken_oaths_prod &gt; legacy_backup.sql
pg_dump -U postgres app_broken_oaths_prod &gt; managed_backup.sql</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="step-title">Stop the app container</p>
          <p>Nothing should write to the empty destination while the restore is in flight.</p>
          <pre><code>docker stop &lt;app-container&gt;</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="step-title">Pipe the legacy dump straight into the destination</p>
          <pre><code>pg_dump --clean --if-exists -U postgres broken_oaths_prod \
  | docker exec -i cms-postgres psql -U postgres -d app_broken_oaths_prod</code></pre>
          <div class="note">Expect — and ignore — <strong><code>ERROR: role "…" does not exist</code></strong> on the ownership statements. The dump references the legacy role name; the actual <code>COPY</code> data statements still apply cleanly underneath.</div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="step-title">Fix table and sequence ownership</p>
          <p>Reassign everything to the destination's real app role.</p>
          <pre><code>SELECT 'ALTER TABLE ' || tablename || ' OWNER TO app_broken_oaths_prod_app;'
FROM pg_tables WHERE schemaname = 'public';

SELECT 'ALTER SEQUENCE ' || sequencename || ' OWNER TO app_broken_oaths_prod_app;'
FROM pg_sequences WHERE schemaname = 'public';</code></pre>
          <p>Run the generated statements, then move on.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="step-title">Restart and verify</p>
          <pre><code>docker start &lt;app-container&gt;
docker logs -f &lt;app-container&gt;</code></pre>
          <p>Look for a clean boot — <strong>"Migrations already up"</strong> is the tell. Confirm row counts match the legacy source, and confirm the app answers <code>200</code>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="step-title">Leave the legacy database alone</p>
          <p>Keep it running, untouched, as a read-only fallback. Deleting it is a separate, later decision — not part of this pass.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="principles" class="phase">
      <div class="phase-head"><span class="phase-tag">Standing</span><h2>Decision principles</h2></div>
      <p class="phase-intro">Rules that applied across every phase above, not just the one they first came up in.</p>

      <ul class="principle-list">
        <li>
          <span class="mark">&#8212;</span>
          <p><strong>SSH access to a box is an account-level concern, never project-level.</strong> One key per account, read by account, not by project — reasoning about "whose key" on a box shared across projects is the wrong frame entirely.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="mark">&#8212;</span>
          <p><strong>Centralize on managed infrastructure — don't perpetuate a legacy instance just because it's already correct today.</strong> Repointing <code>DATABASE_URL</code> back at an unmanaged database is fast, but the next engineer rediscovers the same special case with no record of why it's special. Migrate the data onto the platform's instance instead.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="mark">&#8212;</span>
          <p><strong>Prefer re-running the actual idempotent pipeline over hand-patching a box.</strong> <code>force: true</code> exists precisely so a stale "satisfied" verdict doesn't have to be worked around by SSHing in and improvising.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="mark">&#8212;</span>
          <p><strong>Back up before database surgery, every time, no exceptions.</strong> A dump of both sides took minutes and made every later step reversible.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="compare">
        <div class="compare-card no">
          <h4>Repoint DATABASE_URL back</h4>
          <p>Fast, but a one-off patch. The next deploy — or the next engineer — rediscovers the same unmanaged instance with no record of why it's special.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-card yes">
          <h4>Migrate the data onto cms-postgres</h4>
          <p>Slower once, but the project now lives entirely inside the automation's own naming, backups, and access model — like every project provisioned from scratch.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="gaps" class="phase">
      <div class="phase-head"><span class="phase-tag">Standing</span><h2>Known platform gaps — not fixed here</h2></div>
      <p class="phase-intro">This playbook is the workaround for today. These are the actual code-level fixes worth their own stories on code_my_spec's backlog, so the next migration hits fewer of these blind.</p>
      <ul class="gaplist">
        <li>
          <div class="callout callout-gap">
            <p class="callout-label">verify() can be narrower than run()</p>
            <p>A step's satisfied verdict only covers what its own <code>verify/3</code> checks — not every side effect its <code>run/3</code> performs. <code>force: true</code> is a workaround, not a fix. The real fix is giving a step's verify a way to name <em>which</em> of its side effects it can and can't confirm, so "satisfied" stops silently overclaiming.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <div class="callout callout-gap">
            <p class="callout-label">No pre-flight check for existing unmanaged data</p>
            <p><code>Database.serve/3</code> and <code>write_database_url</code> have no check for "does this project already have real data somewhere unmanaged" before minting a fresh database and overwriting <code>DATABASE_URL</code> on a project's first successful deploy. Worth a pre-flight warning on this exact code path.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <footer>
      Runbook — not a postmortem. Follow it top to bottom on the next migration, and update it the moment any step drifts from what actually works.
    </footer>
  </main>
</div>

</body></html>

